# Deployment Reality Check — YouTube Downloads on Free Cloud Hosting

## The Core Problem

**YouTube aggressively blocks ALL automated requests from cloud datacenter IP addresses** (AWS, GCP, Azure, etc.). This affects:

- ✅ Your app works **locally** because your home internet has a **residential IP** that YouTube trusts
- ❌ **Render**, Railway, Vercel, Heroku — all use datacenter IPs that YouTube blocks
- ❌ **Piped/Invidious** — these YouTube proxy services are also mostly down/blocked by YouTube in 2026
- ❌ No `yt-dlp` client mode (android, web, tv, ios) can bypass this from cloud IPs

> [!IMPORTANT]
> This is NOT a bug in your code — it's a deliberate, well-maintained anti-bot system by YouTube/Google that blocks every free cloud hosting provider.

## What DOES Work From Render

Your app **works perfectly** for non-YouTube platforms from Render:
- ✅ Instagram Reels/Posts
- ✅ TikTok videos
- ✅ Twitter/X videos
- ✅ Facebook videos
- ✅ Reddit, Pinterest, Vimeo, SoundCloud, etc.

## Options to Fix YouTube Downloads

### Option A: Deploy Backend on Oracle Cloud Free Tier (Recommended — FREE Forever)
Oracle Cloud provides **always-free** ARM VMs (4 OCPUs, 24GB RAM). Their IP ranges are less aggressively blocked by YouTube.

1. Sign up at [cloud.oracle.com](https://cloud.oracle.com) (credit card required but never charged)
2. Create an "Always Free" ARM VM (Ampere A1)
3. Install Docker and run your existing `Dockerfile`
4. Point your Vercel frontend's `VITE_BACKEND_URL` to the Oracle VM IP

### Option B: Run Backend Locally + Cloudflare Tunnel (FREE)
Keep the backend on your own PC and expose it to the internet:

```bash
# Install cloudflared
# Then run:
cloudflared tunnel --url http://localhost:3001
```

This gives you a public URL (e.g., `https://xxx.trycloudflare.com`) that your Vercel frontend can connect to. YouTube won't block it because requests come from your residential IP.

### Option C: Accept YouTube Limitation
Keep Render for everything else, and add a note in the UI that YouTube downloads require running the backend locally.

### Option D: Use a Paid Residential Proxy (~$5-20/month)
Add a proxy to yt-dlp: `--proxy socks5://user:pass@proxy.example.com:1080`
Services like [webshare.io](https://webshare.io) offer cheap residential proxies.
